# Creates and removes the service account. A package cannot ship a user, so
# this is the one thing that has to happen at install time rather than in
# package().

_user=peekesp

# --user-group, not left to the distribution's default. The service's files are
# now group-owned (alerts.json, the outbox folder, the endpoint's key), and the
# group is how a non-root user is allowed to send a message, so it has to exist
# under the same name whatever USERGROUPS_ENAB says.
_ensure_user() {
    if ! id "$_user" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        useradd --system --user-group --no-create-home --shell /usr/bin/nologin "$_user"
    fi
}

post_install() {
    _ensure_user
    cat <<'MSG'

PeekESP is installed but not paired. The device shows a code on its screen:

    sudo peekesp pair K7M2-P4QX-9R
    sudo peekesp enable

Then `peekesp status` says whether it is actually pushing, which is not the
same question as whether the service is running.

It can also read GPU, NPU and fan sensors, run alert rules, and carry messages
to your phone: see `peekesp sensors`, `peekesp rules` and `peekesp notify`.

MSG
}

post_upgrade() {
    _ensure_user
    # Not post_install: that tells someone who paired months ago that the
    # package is not paired. What an upgrade does need to say is that the running
    # service is still the old agent, with the old unit, until it is restarted.
    cat <<'MSG'

PeekESP was upgraded. The running service is still the previous version:

    sudo systemctl restart peek-agent

This release can read GPU, NPU and fan sensors, run alert rules, and carry
messages to your phone. `peekesp status` says afterwards if anything is only
half set up.

MSG
}

post_remove() {
    # The account goes; /etc/peekesp/agent.conf does not, because it holds the
    # pairing code and pacman treats it as a backup file. Removing it here
    # would delete a credential the user may still want on reinstall. For the
    # same reason /etc/peekesp/alerts.json stays: it is the person's own rules.
    #
    # What does go is the state directory. pacman does not own it (systemd made
    # it at runtime), and left behind it would be owned by a user id that no
    # longer exists. It holds only the outbox, the history and the endpoint's key,
    # all made again on the next start.
    rm -rf /var/lib/peekesp
    # A drop-in that `peekesp repair` may have added before this unit carried
    # the state directory itself.
    rm -f /etc/systemd/system/peek-agent.service.d/state.conf
    rmdir /etc/systemd/system/peek-agent.service.d 2>/dev/null || true
    userdel "$_user" 2>/dev/null || true
}
